pra: 0199 - repeat the power-key control every prakeyevery=M seconds

0198's single-shot consumer-control pair could not be observed on the device.
Boot ring63 shows the N=300 press landing roughly five minutes after
power-on, while this port needs >=10 minutes before the lock screen exists,
so both presses were spent on a screen with nothing to answer them.
`prakeyevery=M` switches the same timer into repeat mode: after the first
pair at `prapowerkey=N`, it fires one pair every M seconds, alternating the
usage every run (0x30 power, 0xe9 volume-up, 0x30, ...), up to
SWEET_KEY_MAX_PAIRS=240 pairs. At M=30 that is `python3 -c 'print(240*30)'`
= 7200 s = `python3 -c 'print(240*30/3600)'` = 2.0 hours of coverage, and 240
pairs of ~8 log lines = `python3 -c 'print(240*8)'` = 1920 lines, which the
4 MiB pralog ring keeps with room to spare. Without prakeyevery the
behaviour is 0198's exactly: two pairs, 120 s apart, then done. The arm line
now carries `every=%u` so the mode is readable before the first press.

FALSIFIER: expect `SWEET HID: powerkey arm secs=900 every=30 err=0 armed=1
clients=1`, then the 0198 group - `SWEET HID: powerkey N down (interval 900
s) usage 0x30`, `SWEET HID: key 0x30 down (N clients)`, `SWEET HID: event
size=60`, `SWEET HID: key 0x30 up` - and 30 s later the same group with
`usage 0xe9`, then `usage 0x30` again, repeating every 30 s until run 240.
Alternating usages are the point: a power press must show as a display-off
attempt and volume-up as a HUD, so one look at the screen has two chances per
minute. The absence of the `powerkey %u down` group after the first pair, or
a constant usage value, falsifies repeat mode; a negative visible response is
still weaker than a positive because iOS may not map our usage values.

